# 37. Train-angle Hessian and the exact backward-train mechanism

This section records the exact second-variation identities found by projecting the
diameter objective into train-angle coordinates.

Assume for this section a simply connected pure rhombic disk so that standard
train coordinates are available.

Let

\[
u_\tau=(\cos\phi_\tau,\sin\phi_\tau)
\]

be the unit edge vector of train \(\tau\).

For vertices \(i,j\), write

\[
r=p_i-p_j
=
\sum_\tau d_\tau u_\tau,
\qquad
d_\tau\in\{-1,0,1\}.
\]

Let

\[
f_{ij}(\phi)
=
\frac12|r|^2.
\]

## 37.1 Exact Hessian trace

Let

\[
k_{ij}
=
\#\{\tau:d_\tau\ne0\}.
\]

Differentiation with respect to the independent train angles gives

\[
\boxed{
\operatorname{tr}\nabla_\phi^2 f_{ij}
=
k_{ij}-|p_i-p_j|^2.
}
\tag{37.1}
\]

For an active diameter pair,

\[
|p_i-p_j|=D,
\]

hence

\[
\boxed{
\operatorname{tr}\nabla_\phi^2 f_{ij}
=
k_{ij}-D^2
\le
T-D^2
=
-\Theta(D^2).
}
\tag{37.2}
\]

Thus each active diameter function is strongly superharmonic in aggregate on the
train-angle space.

## 37.2 Exact neutral-direction formula

Put

\[
a_\tau=d_\tau u_\tau,
\qquad
\lambda_\tau=r\cdot a_\tau.
\]

For an angle velocity \(v=(v_\tau)\), let

\[
b=\sum_\tau v_\tau J a_\tau.
\]

First-order neutrality for this diameter means

\[
r\cdot b=0.
\]

Equivalently, after resolving the neutral direction relative to \(r\), the exact
second variation can be written in weighted variance form whenever all
\(\lambda_\tau\ge0\):

\[
\boxed{
D^2f_{ij}[v,v]
=
-\sum_\tau
\lambda_\tau
\left(
v_\tau-\bar v
\right)^2
\le0,
}
\tag{37.3}
\]

where

\[
\bar v
=
\frac{\sum_\tau\lambda_\tau v_\tau}{D^2}.
\]

Equality requires common rotation on all positively contributing separating trains.

## 37.3 Backward trains are necessary for positive higher-order locking

A separating train is called **backward** for the diameter pair if

\[
\boxed{
\lambda_\tau
=
d_\tau\,r\cdot u_\tau
<
0.
}
\tag{37.4}
\]

If every separating train is forward, then every first-order-neutral train-angle
direction has nonpositive second variation.

Therefore:

\[
\boxed{
\text{every genuinely positive higher-order diameter blocker must use
a backward train or a diameter-invisible train coordinate.}
}
\tag{37.5}
\]

This is an exact necessary condition.

## 37.4 Coordinate-flip obstruction

If \(a_\tau=d_\tau u_\tau\) is backward, then

\[
|r-a_\tau|^2
=
D^2+1-2r\cdot a_\tau
>
D^2.
\tag{37.6}
\]

Hence the coordinate-flipped extreme vertex cannot exist while all other train
coordinates are held fixed; otherwise the configuration would already contain a
pair longer than \(D\).

This gives the geometric interpretation of backwardness:

> a backward train certifies a missing train-coordinate flip.

The natural next step is to propagate this missing flip along the ordered train to
its terminal boundary/fault structures and charge it without overcounting.

## 37.5 Boundary backtracking bound

For a suitable boundary arc of length \(L\) joining a diameter pair, the total
negative weight obeys

\[
\boxed{
\sum_{\lambda_\tau<0}
(-\lambda_\tau)
\le
\frac D2(L-D).
}
\tag{37.7}
\]

Thus the positive curvature available from backward trains is quantitatively tied
to boundary backtracking.

## 37.6 Pure-disk hull contact degree

At a hull vertex of a pure strict-rhombic disk, if the contact degree is \(d\),
then \(d-1\) rhombi occupy the convex interior angle.

Every rhombic corner is \(>\pi/3\), while the hull interior angle is \(<\pi\).
Therefore

\[
(d-1)\frac\pi3<\pi
\]

and

\[
\boxed{d\le3.}
\tag{37.8}
\]

Thus each diametral hull reaction is transmitted into the rhombic bulk through
only two or three unit bars.

---

# 38. Full first-order train-cusp exclusion theorem

This section incorporates the strongest theorem from the subsequent Sol Pro run.

It should be treated as the new Target B frontier.

## 38.1 Statement

> **Theorem 38.1 (full train-cusp exclusion).**
>
> A sufficiently large triangle-free pure-rhombic-disk global minimizer cannot
> have its entire \((T-1)\)-dimensional train-angle space locked to first order by
> the active diameter constraints.

Equivalently, the active diameter gradients cannot generate a full first-order
cusp on all nontrivial train-angle directions.

## 38.2 First-order locking forces \(q\ge T\)

A full cusp on the \(T-1\)-dimensional train-angle quotient requires at least \(T\)
active diameter gradients.

For a convex polygon on \(q\) hull vertices, the planar diameter graph has at most
\(q\) edges.

Hence full first-order locking implies

\[
\boxed{T\le q.}
\tag{38.1}
\]

Together with

\[
q\le P\le\pi D,
\]

this gives

\[
T\le\pi D.
\]

## 38.3 Lower density of train crossings under a full cusp

In a pure rhombic disk,

\[
n=R+T+1.
\]

Using the strengthened lattice bound

\[
D^2<c(n-1)=c(R+T)
\]

and \(T\le\pi D\), one obtains asymptotically

\[
\boxed{
\liminf\frac{R}{T^2}
\ge
\frac1{c\pi^2}
=
\frac1{2\pi\sqrt3}
=
0.0918881492\ldots.
}
\tag{38.2}
\]

## 38.4 Phase-atomic upper bound

Since

\[
E=O(T),
\]

the total angular defect is only boundary order.

Every positive-density weak limit of the train-crossing measure is therefore
supported on atomic projective phase classes separated by \(60^\circ\).

If \(s\) denotes the total atomic train mass, Mantel's inequality gives

\[
\boxed{
\limsup\frac{R}{T^2}
\le
\frac{s^2}{4}.
}
\tag{38.3}
\]

## 38.5 Exposed-hull argument forces \(s\le1/2\)

Normalized minimizing hulls converge to the Euclidean disk through asymptotic
isodiametric equality.

The outer rhombic boundary has \(2T\) unit edges.

Under the full-cusp assumption \(q\ge T\), at least \(T-o(T)\) bounded-length hull
chains must be exposed.

If more than half the train mass were atomic, these exposed bounded chains would
have normals in only a countable atomic set in the scaling limit.

But the disk's surface-area measure is non-atomic.

Therefore

\[
\boxed{s\le\frac12.}
\tag{38.4}
\]

Consequently,

\[
\boxed{
\limsup\frac{R}{T^2}
\le
\frac1{16}
=
0.0625.
}
\tag{38.5}
\]

This contradicts

\[
\frac1{2\pi\sqrt3}
>
\frac1{16}.
\]

Hence a full first-order cusp is impossible.

## 38.6 What survives

A hypothetical pure jammed survivor must therefore satisfy

\[
\boxed{q<T.}
\tag{38.6}
\]

Its active diameter constraints leave a positive-dimensional complement in
train-angle space at first order.

Every mode in this complement that is nevertheless blocked at higher order must,
by Section 37, involve:

- a backward train; or
- a train coordinate invisible to the loaded diameter support.

Thus the first-order train-angle variational branch is eliminated.

The problem is now genuinely second order.

---

# 39. Sharper train/boundary constants without a full cusp

Even without the full-cusp hypothesis, pinned contact rank yields

\[
\frac qT
\ge
\frac12+o(1).
\tag{39.1}
\]

Combining this with the phase-atom/exposed-hull argument gives

\[
\boxed{
\limsup\frac{R}{T^2}
\le
\frac9{64}.
}
\tag{39.2}
\]

Since

\[
\frac{R}{D^2}\to\frac1c,
\]

every hypothetical pure jammed survivor obeys

\[
\boxed{
\liminf\frac TD
\ge
\frac8{3\sqrt c}
=
2.5395008183\ldots.
}
\tag{39.3}
\]

Therefore

\[
\boxed{
\liminf\frac BD
\ge
5.0790016366\ldots.
}
\tag{39.4}
\]

This improves the earlier train lower bound

\[
\frac TD
\ge
\frac2{\sqrt c}
=
1.9046256137\ldots.
\]

The surviving equality mechanism is now sharply constrained:

\[
\boxed{
\begin{array}{c}
q<T,\\
\text{first-order diameter gradients do not span train-angle space},\\
\text{higher-order locking is required on a positive-dimensional complement},\\
\text{every positive-curvature blocker uses backward or invisible coordinates}.
\end{array}
}
\tag{39.5}
\]

---

# 40. Exact remaining lemma after full-cusp exclusion

The current pure-rhombic-disk endgame is:

> **Backward-train / higher-order shell-rotation lemma.**
>
> In a triangle-free global minimizer satisfying
>
> \[
> G+E<c_0B,
> \]
>
> the active diameter bars cannot second-order-lock every train-angle direction
> outside their first-order gradient span using backward-train incidences or
> train coordinates invisible to the loaded diameter support.

A bridge-forest version must simultaneously prove that the same \(O(D)\)
diametral shell cannot kill \(\omega(D)\) independent forest modes.

The most concrete route is now:

1. for every backward train, use (37.6) to identify the forbidden coordinate flip;
2. propagate the missing flip along the ordered train to its two terminal
   boundary/fault structures;
3. assign a rank-sensitive charge to indentation-pocket slack,
   exceptional-face slack, or ordered missing-crossing fault lines;
4. prove a nonovercounting theorem showing that the number of independent
   backward/invisible modes is strictly smaller than the dimension left
   unspanned by the active diameter gradients whenever
   \[
   G+E<c_0B;
   \]
5. conclude that a full higher-order lock is impossible.

That would force a first-order cusp, contradict Theorem 38.1.

The same nonovercounting mechanism, applied to bridge-tree degrees of freedom,
would close Target A as well.

At present, this rank-sensitive nonovercounting charge is the sole unsupported
step in the most developed affirmative proof architecture.

---

# 41. Updated exact frontier

After Sections 36–40, the pure jammed Target B picture is:

\[
\boxed{
\begin{array}{c}
G+E<c_0B,\\[1mm]
q<T,\\[1mm]
T/D\ge2.5395008183\ldots+o(1),\\[1mm]
B/D\ge5.0790016366\ldots+o(1),\\[1mm]
\text{full first-order train cusp impossible},\\[1mm]
\text{remaining lock must be higher order},\\[1mm]
\text{positive higher-order curvature requires backward or invisible trains}.
\end{array}
}
\tag{41.1}
\]

Thus the next Level I proof should not attempt another first-order train-angle
rank argument.

It must eliminate the backward/invisible higher-order locking mechanism, ideally
with a nonovercounting shell charge that also controls the flexible forest branch.

